  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 129 AM EDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nThe meandering upper low overhead will dissipate and be re-absorbed \ninto the mean westerlies over the next two days, but steady moisture \nand August temperatures should allow for convection to fire each \nafternoon, with medium chances for showers and storms in the lower \nelevations. As our upper low feature dissipates to end the week, \norographic forced storms I think will be more likely than valley \nbased convection when we hit the weekend and head into next week. \nNext work week there's still uncertainty regarding strength of flow \naloft, especially north of our area, but the NBM's low to medium \nchances for PM hours showers and storms looks reasonable, as we wait \nto see where better forcing arrives or if we're left to \norographically forced storms for a longer period of time.\n\nOtherwise each day should have 1000 to 3000 J CAPE available in the \nrelatively moist airmass, and dewpoints in the upper 60s and low 70s \nshould help combine for similar rain rate potential as yesterday. \nDays with lower CAPE may limit the overall risk, but the scattered \nsummer convection should lend itself to a lightning and isolated \nflooding risk. A few gusts to 40 or 50 mph too depending on storm \ndepth. Steering winds are still generally 10 knots or less over the \nnext couple of days, so slow moving storms will have opportunity to \nrack up a quick couple inches of rain. As always with summer \nconvection, location location location is everything with how \nimpactful a flash flood risk is, so be mindful of local low spots or \nstreams that don't need much to cause impacts. Transition to \norographic induced storms over the weekend may also transition the \nbulk of the risk to the terrain for flooding potential, but any \ndecent outflow boundary into the valley could spark new convection, \nas we saw yesterday.",
